{# A node that holds children: `node.html` plus the mount they land in.

   Any node with children renders through this template whether or not it says
   so -- compound-ness is a state, not a type. Say `extends: group` when you
   also want the group *look*, since that puts `node--group` on the wrapper and
   the `.node--group` rules in viewer.css then apply.

   Both parts are load-bearing, and neither failure is loud:
     .node__header    -- engine/collapse.js only wires a collapse toggle when a
                         node has one (it comes from node.html).
     .node__children  -- where the engine mounts child nodes. Omit it and the
                         engine appends its own at the end, which is usually
                         *not* where you wanted it.

   The matching CSS is not guessable either -- children are positioned by ELK
   relative to this node's origin, so the wrapper must not pad or cap them:

       .node--TYPE                  { padding: 0; max-width: none; }
       .node--TYPE > .node__header  { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0;
                                      height: var(--header-h); }
       .node--TYPE > .node__children{ position: absolute; inset: 0; }

   (`--header-h` is the single source of truth for header height -- layout
   padding, drag clamping and collapse all read it. Don't hardcode it.) #}
{% extends "node.html" %}
{% block children %}<div class="node__children"></div>{% endblock %}
